  what fraction of spikes at the larynx follow another within:
    arm         5       10      20      40      80      ms
    off         0.1     2.1     9.5     21.2    36.2    
    burst       0.1     2.1     9.6     21.4    36.7    
    burst+tuft  1.2     5.4     14.8    26.7    41.2    

  burst% is burst spikes over all spikes at the larynx. burst|plat and
  burst|no split that by whether the neuron's tuft was in a plateau, and
  their difference is whether feedback can steer the sign of learning.
  obj|burst classifies cube against ball from the per-neuron burst
  DEVIATION — the exact quantity the weight update multiplies.

  off arm burst%         0.000   (must be 0 — the control)
  burst code             9.6%    (must be inside 1-95%)
  plateau occupancy      40.4%    (must be inside 0.5-95%)
  burst|plat vs |no      18.3% vs 8.1%   (the tuft must raise it)
  burst vs plateau       0.500 vs 0.920   <- the burst LOSES specificity vs the plateau it comes from

  PASS — the burst code runs and the tuft steers it.

  WHAT IT BUYS, and the plateau column is the honest reading of it:
  the per-neuron burst deviation at the larynx carries cube-versus-ball
  at 0.500 against a 32-permutation null of 0.487. That is a third factor
  that DISCRIMINATES, which no previous one here did — `eligprobe` reads
  the trace R-STDP multiplies as object-blind, +0.93 correlated between
  the two conditions.

  But the plateau it is derived from reads 0.920, and the burst reads
  0.500. Turning a plateau into a burst rate is a nonlinearity applied to
  a signal that was already there, and it loses. DNA v29 had the 0.920
  signal and could not teach with it, because a gate can only attenuate;
  v37's claim is that a SIGN is worth more than the specificity it
  costs. That claim is not settled by this probe — it needs `m3`.
  [burstprobe took 16.3 s]

