  the last two columns are the SAME module: spike counts against the
  nine articulator centroids, minus the two v51 steers. `vocab` already
  measured that the knobs beat the neurons on this module, and the gap
  here is the same finding arriving from a different direction.

  THE ARTICULATOR READOUT, in M1b's own window (1100-1500), on the
  seven groups v51 does NOT steer: 0.789 (shuffled 0.497)

  shuffled control in a REWARD bin: auditory 0.469, central 0.468,
  vocal-minus-F1/F2 0.486
  worst reward bin:  auditory 0.541, central 0.503, vocal spikes 0.588
                     ARTICULATORS 0.740 +/- 0.040 per creature (bar 0.75)
                     M1b's own window 0.789

  IT CANNOT, AND THE REASON IS TIMING RATHER THAN LEGIBILITY.
  The word is legible while it plays -- 1.000 in auditory in the first
  bin -- and by the reward window the best ANY of them manages is
  0.742, against the 0.75 a two-context index needs. The voice does not
  carry it either, so M1b's echo is not a usable memory here.

  So what is missing is not a better readout of the ear. It is somewhere
  to HOLD the context across the silence between hearing a word and
  being rewarded for answering it, and this creature has nowhere: no
  module here holds a kick for 10 ms, and an utterance is a held vowel
  rather than a trajectory.

  That is a sharper statement of what stands between v51 and naming than
  anything the conditioning work produced, and it names a mechanism
  class rather than a tuning knob: persistent activity.
  [ctxsrc took 129.6 s]

