  ctxaxis -- the oracle chain proved the DERIVED INDEX caused the F1 bit's
  decay and that a perfect index REVERSES it. This asks the next question
  down: is F1 worse than F2 because the EAR does not separate 118 Hz, or
  because the CLUSTERING RULE throws the separation away?
  the feature   `feat_ema`, the ear's rate EMA at reward time -- the exact
                vector DNA v53 source 4 clusters, on kGridWords' 2x2 grid.
  the scoring   one BIT at a time, held out, with any slot-to-bit map fitted
                on the training half only. So chance is exactly 0.50 and the
                F1-minus-F2 difference has a null of exactly ZERO.
  the columns   ear (a supervised READ of the feature, not an absolute
                ceiling -- it fits 2048 parameters where a partition fits 4),
                kern (the kernel's own rule, from the origin), raw (seeded
                from data: isolates INIT), z (per-channel SCALE), sph
                (overall MAGNITUDE).
  the primary   bit F1 minus bit F2, paired per creature, on `ear` and on
                `kern`. An asymmetric ear means RESOLUTION; a symmetric ear
                with an asymmetric kernel means the RULE, and is actionable.
  read-only     no learning, no genome field, nothing shipped.
  18 creatures, 4 slots, 8 inits per seeded column.

  usable rows per creature 393.9 of 428 trials the budget buys (the rest were slept through).

  column   n     bit F1           bit F2           F1 - F2 paired
  ear       18   0.834 +/- 0.024    0.964 +/- 0.004    -0.130 +/- 0.024 (5.3 SE)
  ear-z     18   0.834 +/- 0.024    0.964 +/- 0.004    -0.130 +/- 0.024 (5.3 SE)
  ear-sph   18   0.637 +/- 0.023    0.955 +/- 0.014    -0.318 +/- 0.029 (11.1 SE)
  kern      18   0.837 +/- 0.008    0.843 +/- 0.007    -0.006 +/- 0.012 (0.5 SE)
  raw       18   0.848 +/- 0.009    0.737 +/- 0.014    +0.111 +/- 0.022 (5.2 SE)
  z         18   0.905 +/- 0.011    0.796 +/- 0.010    +0.109 +/- 0.010 (10.7 SE)
  sph       18   0.939 +/- 0.008    0.918 +/- 0.010    +0.021 +/- 0.007 (2.9 SE)
