  VOID CHECK, shuffled labels on `ear`: 0.505 +/- 0.009 (F1), 0.492 +/- 0.008 (F2)
                against a constructed chance of 0.500.

  THE PRIMARY, as registered. ear F1-F2 -0.130 +/- 0.024 (5.3 SE);  kern F1-F2 -0.006 +/- 0.012 (0.5 SE)

  THE REGISTERED RESOLUTION BRANCH FIRES: the supervised reader gets the
  F1 bit out of the ear worse than the F2 bit, 0.834 against 0.964, 5.3 SE
  on a quantity whose null is exactly zero.

  AND IT CANNOT BE READ AS A RESOLUTION LIMIT. THE PRE-REGISTRATION WAS
  MIS-SPECIFIED, and the table refutes it from inside.

  WHY: every column is a DECODER scored held-out, so every column is a
  LOWER BOUND on what the feature holds, and two bounds cannot be
  subtracted. The table shows that directly -- `ear-sph` reads bit F1 at
  0.637 where `ear` reads 0.834 on the SAME feature, so that reader lost
  0.197 of content and the apparent asymmetry GREW to 11.1 SE. An asymmetry
  that moves with the reader is a fact about readers.

  WHAT IT CAN SAY: the feature holds bit F1 at AT LEAST 0.939 (sph) and
  bit F2 at AT LEAST 0.964 (ear). Those bounds differ by 0.025, where the
  reader-based "asymmetries" above read 0.115 and 0.305. The resolution
  question is therefore NOT SETTLED by this run, and must not be cited as
  settled -- 118 Hz being harder than 300 Hz remains plausible and
  unmeasured. Settling it needs a CEILING, not another decoder.

  WHAT THE PARTITION LOSES against the best supervised read of the same
  feature (ear), per bit:
    bit F1   reader 0.834, kernel 0.837, best partition 0.939  (gap +0.105)
    bit F2   reader 0.964, kernel 0.843, best partition 0.918  (gap -0.046)

  AND WHICH COMPONENT, one variable at a time (bit F1 then bit F2):
    bit F1  kern 0.837 -> raw 0.848 (INIT)  -> z 0.905 (per-channel SCALE)  -> sph 0.939 (overall MAGNITUDE)
    bit F2  kern 0.843 -> raw 0.737 (INIT)  -> z 0.796 (per-channel SCALE)  -> sph 0.918 (overall MAGNITUDE)
    the supervised reads of the same feature: raw 0.834/0.964, z 0.834/0.964,
    L2 0.637/0.955 (F1/F2). `ear-z` is a NO-OP by construction and is a wiring
    check rather than a column -- `holdout_accuracy` standardises every
    dimension on the training half itself, so handing it a pre-z-scored
    feature must reproduce `ear` exactly, and it does.

  THE DECISION STATISTIC, paired per creature. `sph-0` minus `kern` is ONE
  variable -- the kernel's own zero-init rule with the overall MAGNITUDE
  removed and nothing else -- and is what licenses the build. `sph` minus
  `kern` is printed too and is NOT the licensing number: it bundles the
  normalisation with a DATA-SEEDED init the kernel does not do.
    bit F1   sph-0 - kern +0.007 +/- 0.012 (0.5 SE)   under 3 SE
            reference:  sph - kern +0.102 +/- 0.012 (8.7 SE, BUNDLED),  sph - raw +0.091 +/- 0.011 (8.1 SE)
    bit F2   sph-0 - kern -0.019 +/- 0.009 (-2.1 SE)   under 3 SE
            reference:  sph - kern +0.075 +/- 0.010 (7.2 SE, BUNDLED),  sph - raw +0.181 +/- 0.016 (11.0 SE)

  THE MAGNITUDE LEAD IS NOT LICENSED at this n: one of the two bits does
  not clear 3 SE paired, and a transform that helps one bit while leaving
  the other unresolved is not the clean win the column means suggest.

  THE VIGILANCE SWEEP -- the kernel's only source of DISTINCT data-driven
  seeds, which is what `sph-0` showed the normalisation needs. Each cell is
  zero-init plus the ART test; `slots` is how many ever committed of 4 and
