  question          `blockanchor` says freezing the UPPER half of F1 costs 53%
                    of the lesson and the LOWER half 9%. My leverage law was
                    FITTED to three one-neuron costs and FAILS the two
                    half-block costs from the same run: it predicts a ratio of
                    3.48 where 5.89 was measured, and no centroid position
                    reaches 5.89 (the maximum over all v is 4.31).
  what is measured  the two quantities that law ASSUMED -- the centroid's own
                    position and the per-neuron weights -- and, the point of
                    the run, how much each neuron's rate actually MOVES while
                    the lesson is learned. That needs no fitted constant.
  the prior         `stageprobe` says IP drives every neuron toward the SAME
                    rate, which flattens exactly this profile. A FLAT answer
                    here is the expected one and it REFUSES the rate account
                    as well as the position account.

