  ARM LIVENESS vs `w100` (seeds where the arm differs at all)
    w75                36/36
    w50                36/36
    w25                36/36

  WHAT THE MASK COSTS THE LESSON (err taught vs `w100`, paired on seed).
  The unmasked creature learns 0.1676 of error (1.0080 -> 0.8404); a cost near
  that figure means the lesson is gone rather than merely reduced.
  arm      reachable  cost in err taught     share of the lesson lost
  w75      62        % +0.1190 +/- 0.0102       71%
  w50      50        % +0.1118 +/- 0.0092       67%
  w25      38        % +0.1128 +/- 0.0112       67%

  SHAPE CHECKS
    cost spread across the widths  6%   (flat means the damage is not
                                          about width)
    worst arm's err taught         0.9594  vs err_before 1.0080

  CONFINEMENT IS REFUSED, BUT NOT FOR THE REASON THIS GATE WAS BUILT ON.

  THE EMPIRICAL PART STANDS: every width costs most of the lesson. Blocking
  even a QUARTER of the F1 group (w75) costs 59% of what the unmasked
  creature learned, so there is no width that both leaves room for a second
  lesson and lets the first one learn. Write separation by restricting
  plasticity is refused on this protocol.

  THE RANGE-CAP EXPLANATION IS REFUTED BY THIS RUN'S OWN SHAPE. If the cost
  were the centroid's range being partitioned, loss would track 1 - reachable
  -- 38% / 50% / 62% -- and the curve would be steep. Observed is 59% /
  58% / 68%: higher everywhere and far flatter. A quarter blocked costs
  nearly as much as three quarters blocked, which no range argument predicts.

  WHAT THE SHAPE DOES POINT AT. A blocked neuron is not removed from the
  creature -- it still receives exploration noise, still fires, and is STILL
  READ BY THE CENTROID. It simply never cashes in. So blocking does not hand
  the lesson a smaller instrument; it leaves the same instrument with part of
  it wired to noise, diluting the readout and adding variance. That is
  consistent with `variance, not credit`, and it is why a quarter is nearly
  as bad as three quarters.

  THE STRUCTURAL CLAIM, stated as what this supports: a POOLED readout cannot
  be partitioned by restricting plasticity alone, because the pool still
  includes what you excluded from learning. Separating two lessons would
  require the READOUT to ignore the other lesson's neurons too, which a
  rate-weighted centroid over a fixed group cannot do.

  THE TEST THAT WOULD SETTLE IT: silence the blocked neurons' exploration
  noise as well as their cash-in. If the cost falls toward 1 - reachable, the
  dilution account is right; if it stays flat, something else is.
  [maskretain took 7241.2 s]

