  --- imitate, 560000 ticks ---
experiment: imitate
  session           560.0 s x 5 creatures, two words to an EMPTY
                    FIELD, trial order shuffled
  instrument        imitate   trial seed 00000000013533de   200 trials each
                    another probe is another trial sequence: its numbers
                    are not comparable with these (~0.05 at 300 trials)
  the question      the word STOPS. Does the voice still carry which
                    one it was? A voice that differs while the sound is
                    still playing is the arcuate transmitting; repeating
                    is what survives the sound stopping.

  window                 voice       articulators shuffled    audible d'  EAR still knows
  WHILE the word plays   0.844       0.734       0.503       1.31        1.000
  0-200 ms after         0.874       0.790       0.488       1.67        0.992
  200-600 ms after       0.838       0.752       0.494       1.70        0.624   <- SCORED
  600-1400 ms after      0.706       0.640       0.498       1.25        0.520
  1400-2300 (ambient)    0.560       0.546       0.500       0.38        0.498   <- ambient babble

  the answer against ambient babble, same trials:
    articulators   burst 0.752  vs ambient 0.546   +0.206
    voice          burst 0.838  vs ambient 0.560   +0.278
    THE ANSWER IS INFORMATIVE — the burst carries the word better
    than the babble the same creature produces unprompted.

    'voice' is the nine motor groups plus loudness and voicing.
    'articulators' drops loudness and voicing entirely, so it cannot
    pass on "one word makes it louder" -- it is a claim about two
    SOUNDS rather than two amounts of sound.
    'audible d'' is the bias-corrected cepstral ruler: 1.0 is roughly
    76% correct for a listener.
    'EAR still knows' is the auditory module on the same trials in the
    same window. It is the control that makes this a claim about
    repeating rather than about hearing: in the scored window the
    stimulus is gone from the ear and still present in the voice.

  M1b PASS — 200-600 ms after the word stops, with the auditory module
  down to 0.624, a held-out classifier still reads which word the
  creature heard off its own voice at 84% (chance 50%, bar 75%,
  the same bar G3 is scored against). 4 of 5 creatures at or above
  it. Audible d' 1.70 against the 1.0 a listener needs.
  Read it beside G3, same creature and same bar: it repeats at 84%
  and names at 53%. The object reaches the larynx and does not reach
  the voice.

