  g3probe is a bound, not a criterion. It reads 0.54 against a 0.75 bar:
  a perfect teacher cannot make this voice depend on what the creature
  sees. Read that against the calibration echo above: the same features
  and the same classifier take the word-driven voice at ~0.83, so the
  readout is not the limit and the null is not a broken pipeline. What
  is missing is a mechanism that can learn a *conditional* mapping from
  what is in view to what the larynx does. Node perturbation cannot: a
  per-neuron bias is a constant, not a function of the input. That
  leaves reward-modulated STDP on central->vocal as the only candidate
  in the creature, and this says it does not manage it even when the
  reward is ideal.

  But weigh that against the condition table above before charging the
  learning rule for all of it. The teacher is ideal; what the mapping
  has to key on is only partly so — read the two losses off the
  condition table rather than assuming which one is larger. At 141
  probes they are vision 0.927 -> central 0.724 (-0.203) and then
  central -> voice (-0.237), so the larger loss is the downstream one,
  which is where m3probe and projprobe both localise it. At the default
  tick count this same table read central at 0.475 and pointed upstream
  instead; that estimate was resolution-limited, not a finding.
  [g3probe took 260.7 s]

